'''wing''' (wing), ''n.'' [Formerly also ''weng''; < ME. ''winge'', ''wenge'', also (with intrusive ''h'') ''hwinge'', ''whenge'', < Icel. ''vængr'' = Sw. Dan. ''vinge'', a wing. The AS. word for ‘wing’ was ''fether''; cf. L. ''penna'', Gr. πτερόν, wing, from the same ult. source: see ''feather'' and ''pen''<sup>2</sup>.] '''1.''' In ''vertebrate zoöl.'', the fore limb, anterior extremity, or appendage of the scapular arch or shoulder-girdle, corresponding to the human arm, fitted in any way for flight or aërial locomotion; or the same limb, however rudimentary or functionless, of a member of a class of animals which ordinarily have this limb fitted for flight. {{smaller|That modification of a limb which makes it a wing occurs in several}}
